1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,649
He went fishing in November 2012.

2
00:00:05,099 --> 00:00:09,820
He would not see land again for 438 days.

3
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:13,881
No working engine. No way to call for
help.

4
00:00:14,331 --> 00:00:18,154
Just a small fishing boat, and the largest
ocean on Earth.

5
00:00:19,054 --> 00:00:23,833
When he finally reached a remote island on
the other side of the Pacific, the story

6
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:26,367
he told seemed almost impossible to
believe.

7
00:00:26,817 --> 00:00:30,030
His name is José Salvador Alvarenga.

8
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:35,242
This is what we know about his journey,
what can be checked, and what rests on his

9
00:00:35,242 --> 00:00:35,827
word alone.

10
00:00:39,527 --> 00:00:44,334
Alvarenga was born in El Salvador, in the
coastal village of Garita Palmera.

11
00:00:44,784 --> 00:00:49,993
In the early 2000s he moved to Mexico, and
found work on the Pacific coast of

12
00:00:49,993 --> 00:00:54,797
Chiapas, fishing for sharks and other
large fish from small open boats.

13
00:00:55,247 --> 00:00:58,510
He had not been in touch with his family
back home for years.

14
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:04,872
On November 17, 2012, he set out from the
fishing village of Costa Azul, near

15
00:01:04,872 --> 00:01:05,716
Pijijiapan.

16
00:01:06,166 --> 00:01:11,888
The plan was ordinary: a long shift at
sea, perhaps thirty hours, and then home.

17
00:01:12,338 --> 00:01:14,319
His usual partner could not come.

18
00:01:14,769 --> 00:01:20,504
In his place came a younger man, Ezequiel
Córdoba, whom Alvarenga barely knew.

19
00:01:20,954 --> 00:01:26,494
Alvarenga was a seasoned fisherman.
Córdoba, by most accounts, had very little

20
00:01:26,494 --> 00:01:27,773
experience at sea.

21
00:01:28,223 --> 00:01:33,317
Their boat was a seven-meter fiberglass
skiff, open to the sky, with a single

22
00:01:33,317 --> 00:01:36,493
outboard motor and a large icebox for the
catch.

23
00:01:40,193 --> 00:01:42,688
Not long after they left, the weather
turned.

24
00:01:43,138 --> 00:01:47,849
According to Alvarenga, a storm pushed
them far from the coast and battered the

25
00:01:47,849 --> 00:01:48,684
boat for days.

26
00:01:49,134 --> 00:01:52,987
The engine failed. Much of their equipment
was damaged or lost.

27
00:01:53,687 --> 00:01:57,058
Without its engine, the open skiff was at
the mercy of the waves.

28
00:01:57,508 --> 00:02:02,737
To keep the boat stable, they threw their
catch, hundreds of kilograms of fish, back

29
00:02:02,737 --> 00:02:03,546
into the sea.

30
00:02:03,996 --> 00:02:09,859
Alvarenga later said he managed one radio
call to his boss before the battery died.

31
00:02:10,309 --> 00:02:14,917
Mexican rescue officials confirmed that a
fishing boat was reported missing from

32
00:02:14,917 --> 00:02:16,990
Costa Azul two days after it sailed.

33
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:22,274
A search began. It was called off after
two days, in poor visibility.

34
00:02:22,724 --> 00:02:27,122
By then, the skiff was already being
carried into the open Pacific.

35
00:02:27,922 --> 00:02:33,941
No sail. No oars. No anchor. Nothing to
steer with but the wind and the current.

36
00:02:37,641 --> 00:02:42,536
Within days, the danger was no longer the
storm. It was food, and water.

37
00:02:42,986 --> 00:02:47,591
Alvarenga, the experienced fisherman,
learned to catch fish with his bare hands

38
00:02:47,591 --> 00:02:49,282
as they swam beside the boat.

39
00:02:49,732 --> 00:02:53,528
He caught seabirds that landed to rest. He
caught sea turtles.

40
00:02:53,978 --> 00:02:58,674
They pulled plastic bottles and other
debris from the water, and turned them

41
00:02:58,674 --> 00:03:00,280
into tools and containers.

42
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:05,126
Sometimes, he later said, they found
scraps of food floating past.

43
00:03:05,576 --> 00:03:09,836
When rain came, the two men collected it
in anything that would hold water.

44
00:03:10,286 --> 00:03:15,805
When it did not, they drank what they
could, including, he later said, turtle

45
00:03:15,805 --> 00:03:16,235
blood.

46
00:03:16,685 --> 00:03:21,903
Every rainstorm mattered. Every catch was
measured against the next empty day.

47
00:03:22,353 --> 00:03:27,250
The icebox, built to keep fish cold,
became their shelter from the sun and the

48
00:03:27,250 --> 00:03:27,627
waves.

49
00:03:28,077 --> 00:03:32,321
On a boat with almost nothing, it was the
closest thing they had to a roof.

50
00:03:36,021 --> 00:03:38,503
The ordeal affected the two men
differently.

51
00:03:38,953 --> 00:03:44,180
According to Alvarenga, Córdoba struggled
with the raw food from the beginning, and

52
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:44,873
became ill.

53
00:03:45,323 --> 00:03:48,031
As the weeks became months, he lost hope.

54
00:03:48,481 --> 00:03:52,947
About four months into the drift, by
Alvarenga's account, Córdoba stopped

55
00:03:52,947 --> 00:03:53,375
eating.

56
00:03:54,275 --> 00:03:55,875
And then he died.

57
00:03:56,975 --> 00:04:01,775
There were no witnesses. Everything we
know about those weeks comes from the man

58
00:04:01,775 --> 00:04:02,855
who survived them.

59
00:04:03,305 --> 00:04:08,328
Alvarenga has said he kept talking to his
companion for days, before he finally let

60
00:04:08,328 --> 00:04:09,841
his body go into the sea.

61
00:04:10,291 --> 00:04:14,959
He later said that he, too, thought about
ending his life, and that his faith held

62
00:04:14,959 --> 00:04:15,471
him back.

63
00:04:15,921 --> 00:04:17,906
From that day on, he was alone.

64
00:04:21,606 --> 00:04:24,077
Alone, Alvarenga built routines.

65
00:04:24,877 --> 00:04:28,695
He fished. He collected rain. He slept in
the icebox.

66
00:04:29,395 --> 00:04:35,251
He kept track of time not with a calendar,
but with the moon, counting its cycles,

67
00:04:35,251 --> 00:04:36,536
one after another.

68
00:04:36,986 --> 00:04:42,283
He said he saw large container ships pass
in the distance. He waved and shouted.

69
00:04:42,283 --> 00:04:43,673
None of them stopped.

70
00:04:44,123 --> 00:04:49,825
He dreamed of food, and of his family. He
talked to himself, and he prayed.

71
00:04:50,275 --> 00:04:56,015
Days of flat calm were followed by
squalls. The sun burned by day.

72
00:04:56,465 --> 00:05:01,208
Thousands of kilometers from any coast, he
was far beyond the reach of any search.

73
00:05:01,658 --> 00:05:06,988
At night, the sky was full of stars, and
there was nothing between him and them.

74
00:05:07,438 --> 00:05:12,177
Week after week, the current carried the
skiff farther west, across the widest part

75
00:05:12,177 --> 00:05:13,033
of the Pacific.

76
00:05:16,733 --> 00:05:19,810
When the story became public, many people
doubted it.

77
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:24,698
Could a man really survive more than a
year in an open boat?

78
00:05:25,498 --> 00:05:30,329
Skeptics asked how anyone could avoid
scurvy for so long, without fruit or

79
00:05:30,329 --> 00:05:31,047
vegetables.

80
00:05:31,497 --> 00:05:37,535
A physiologist at Duke University, Claude
Piantadosi, explained that fresh meat from

81
00:05:37,535 --> 00:05:43,214
birds and turtles contains vitamin C,
enough to prevent the disease if eaten in

82
00:05:43,214 --> 00:05:43,861
quantity.

83
00:05:44,311 --> 00:05:49,472
Some details were hard to check, and a
Mexican report from 2012 even listed the

84
00:05:49,472 --> 00:05:51,562
fishermen under different names.

85
00:05:52,012 --> 00:05:57,748
His parents explained that in Mexico he
was known by another name: Cirilo.

86
00:05:58,398 --> 00:06:00,863
Scientists approached the question
differently.

87
00:06:01,663 --> 00:06:06,452
At the University of Hawaiʻi,
oceanographers Nikolai Maximenko and Jan

88
00:06:06,452 --> 00:06:10,351
Hafner used a computer model of winds and
ocean currents.

89
00:06:10,801 --> 00:06:15,091
They released virtual drifters off the
coast of Chiapas, and followed them for

90
00:06:15,091 --> 00:06:16,026
more than a year.

91
00:06:16,476 --> 00:06:21,351
The drifters moved west in a remarkably
narrow path, toward the Marshall Islands.

92
00:06:21,801 --> 00:06:25,931
The researchers said Alvarenga's account
fell well within the model's limits.

93
00:06:26,381 --> 00:06:31,354
An oceanographer in Australia, Erik van
Sebille, also said it was entirely

94
00:06:31,354 --> 00:06:36,328
possible for currents to carry a boat from
Mexico to the Marshall Islands.

95
00:06:36,778 --> 00:06:41,894
In April 2014, Alvarenga's lawyer said he
had passed a polygraph test about the

96
00:06:41,894 --> 00:06:42,347
voyage.

97
00:06:42,797 --> 00:06:47,563
None of this proves every detail of his
story. But it shows that a journey like

98
00:06:47,563 --> 00:06:49,252
his was physically possible.

99
00:06:52,952 --> 00:06:59,377
On January 30, 2014, after counting more
than fifteen moons, Alvarenga saw land.

100
00:07:00,277 --> 00:07:04,688
It was a tiny islet called Tile, at the
edge of Ebon Atoll in the Marshall

101
00:07:04,688 --> 00:07:08,623
Islands, around ten thousand kilometers
from where he had started.

102
00:07:09,073 --> 00:07:11,141
He left the boat and swam to shore.

103
00:07:11,941 --> 00:07:15,736
He came ashore near a small house
belonging to a local couple.

104
00:07:16,186 --> 00:07:21,690
Two local residents, Emi Libokmeto and
Russel Laikidrik, found him exhausted and

105
00:07:21,690 --> 00:07:23,066
barely able to walk.

106
00:07:23,516 --> 00:07:27,082
They took care of him, and the news
reached the authorities.

107
00:07:27,532 --> 00:07:31,831
Days later he was brought to the capital,
Majuro, for medical care.

108
00:07:32,281 --> 00:07:39,686
438 days after leaving Mexico, José
Salvador Alvarenga was back among people.

109
00:07:43,386 --> 00:07:46,548
Within hours, his story was spreading
around the world.

110
00:07:46,998 --> 00:07:51,781
Doctors found him dehydrated, with low
blood pressure and swollen ankles, but in

111
00:07:51,781 --> 00:07:53,933
better condition than many expected.

112
00:07:54,383 --> 00:07:58,489
An American embassy official who
questioned him said he found him truthful.

113
00:07:58,939 --> 00:08:04,347
On February 10, 2014, he flew home to El
Salvador, to a family that had not heard

114
00:08:04,347 --> 00:08:05,549
from him in years.

115
00:08:05,999 --> 00:08:09,937
In Garita Palmera, his parents had long
feared he was dead.

116
00:08:10,387 --> 00:08:15,799
His daughter told reporters that the first
thing she would do was hug him and kiss

117
00:08:15,799 --> 00:08:16,063
him.

118
00:08:16,513 --> 00:08:21,069
In the months that followed, he struggled
with sleep, and with a new fear of the

119
00:08:21,069 --> 00:08:21,297
sea.

120
00:08:21,747 --> 00:08:29,058
In 2015, the journalist Jonathan Franklin
told his story in detail in the book 438

121
00:08:29,058 --> 00:08:29,504
Days.

122
00:08:29,954 --> 00:08:34,381
Some questions about the voyage can only
ever be answered by Alvarenga himself.

123
00:08:38,081 --> 00:08:44,017
José Salvador Alvarenga survived because
he kept doing small things, again and

124
00:08:44,017 --> 00:08:44,474
again.

125
00:08:44,924 --> 00:08:48,549
Catch the fish. Collect the rain. Count
the moons.

126
00:08:49,449 --> 00:08:51,345
But survival had a cost.

127
00:08:52,145 --> 00:08:55,992
He lost his companion. He lost more than a
year of his life.

128
00:08:56,442 --> 00:09:03,385
And he came home a different man, from an
ocean that had tried, for 438 days, to

129
00:09:03,385 --> 00:09:04,166
keep him.

130
00:09:04,616 --> 00:09:08,845
Some parts of his story can be checked.
Some never will be.

131
00:09:09,745 --> 00:09:15,203
What remains is a man, a small boat, and
an ocean that is still, for most of us,

132
00:09:15,203 --> 00:09:16,499
unimaginably large.
